  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nMidday infrared satellite imagery and mesoanalysis showed a\nstrong moisture plume continuing across western Kansas, with\nwidespread midlevel clouds, and precipitable water in excess \nof 1.5 inches. Any instability was limited to areas southwest \nof Dodge City, where CAPE was climbing above 1000 J/kg, and this\nis where 12z NAM and many CAMs redevelop additional showers and\nthunderstorms this afternoon, spreading northeast this evening.\nMarginally severe storms with hail to 1 inch and wind gusts to \n60 mph are possible, but locally heavy rainfall will continue to\nbe the primary risk given the unseasonably wet atmosphere. \nGiven very heavy rainfall across some of the western zones last\nnight (many 2-4 inch reports) will need to watch for hydrology \nissues through tonight where heavy rain falls on saturated soils.\nWidespread stratus will develop once again with the moist \nboundary layer through Friday morning.\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms will continue Friday and\nFriday night, as the primary synoptic trough approaches, and\ninteracts with the continued moist atmosphere. It will not rain\nthe entire time, but chance to likely category pops from the \nNBM were accepted. Just like Thursday, strong to marginally\nsevere storms are probable, with severe magnitude and coverage\nbeing strongly limited by poor lapse rates/instability and weak\nbulk wind shear. Temperatures will remain seasonably mild \nFriday afternoon in the 70s, held down by the clouds and \nlimited sunshine. \n\nMidlevel ridging is forecast to build over the plains Saturday\nand Sunday, drying out SW KS, and allowing for warmer afternoon\ntemperatures in the 80s. There may be a storm in the vicinity of\nBarber county late Saturday, but most of SW KS will be dry this\nweekend. \n\nWe will be closely monitoring for a potentially significant\nheavy rain/flooding event around the Tuesday/Wednesday time\nframe. Synoptically, a strong western trough is forecast by the\nglobal models to develop across western North America, which is\nexpected to direct remnant tropical moisture from Pacific\nHurricane Polo northeastward into the plains. Some recent model\nruns suggest the combination of strong diffluence/synoptic lift\ncombined with very high tropical moisture may lead to \nwidespread heavy rain and/or thunderstorms Tuesday through \nWednesday. Daylight Monday appears dry, with scattered showers \nand thunderstorms returning Monday night. NBM pops for Tuesday \nare already in the likely category, and further pop increases \nare expected as models latch onto this expected synoptic \nevolution.",
